<h2 id="training" class="scroll-mt-24">Training &amp; Qualifications</h2>
<p><strong>What a dentist is and the scope of general dentistry.</strong> A dentist in India holds a Bachelor of Dental Surgery (BDS) — a five-year undergraduate degree that covers dental anatomy, oral pathology, periodontology, conservative dentistry, prosthodontics, and basic oral surgery. General dentists are the first line of care for teeth and gums. Their scope is wide but mostly non-surgical: check-ups, scaling, fillings, root canals, crowns and bridges, removable dentures, simple extractions, night guards for grinding, and routine cosmetic work like whitening and veneers. They are trained to recognise pathology, restore function, and refer complex problems to a specialist when they exceed the scope of a general practice.</p>
<p><strong>What a maxillofacial surgeon is.</strong> A maxillofacial surgeon is a specialist who diagnoses and surgically treats diseases, injuries, and defects of the head, face, jaws, mouth, and neck. In India, the standard pathway is a five-year BDS followed by a three-year Master of Dental Surgery (MDS) in Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery. Some surgeons take a dual-degree route — MBBS plus MS (General Surgery) or MCh (Head and Neck Surgery) — which broadens their exposure to general surgery, anaesthesia, and intensive care. After MDS, many surgeons complete fellowships in trauma, oncology, cleft, or cosmetic surgery to subspecialise further.</p>
<p><strong>Why maxillofacial surgeons often have medical training.</strong> The face and jaws sit at the boundary of dentistry, medicine, and surgery. Operating on the jaw means working near the airway, the eyes, major blood vessels, and cranial nerves. A maxillofacial surgeon must understand general anaesthesia, fluid management, trauma resuscitation, ICU care, and post-operative medical management — not just dental anatomy. That is why dual-degree and MBBS-entry pathways exist, and why maxillofacial surgery residencies include rotations in general surgery, anaesthesia, ENT, plastic surgery, and emergency medicine. This combined training is what allows a maxillofacial surgeon to manage a pan-facial fracture or an oral cancer resection with reconstruction, which is well outside the scope of a general dentist.</p>
<div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-surface-container-high my-10"><img alt="Maxillofacial surgery team reviewing a CT scan of the jaw" class="w-full h-full object-cover" src="https://picsum.photos/1200/675?random=den-surgeon-2"/></div>
<h2 id="scope" class="scroll-mt-24">Scope of Practice</h2>
<p>The scope of a maxillofacial surgeon is broad and covers the hard and soft tissues of the face, jaws, oral cavity, and neck. Practically, this includes impacted teeth and complex extractions, dental implants with bone grafting, orthognathic (jaw-correcting) surgery, treatment of cysts and tumours of the jaw, oral cancer surgery with reconstruction, facial fracture management, TMJ surgery, salivary-gland surgery, cleft lip and palate repair, and cosmetic procedures tied to the facial skeleton. Anything that needs the operating theatre, general anaesthesia, or reconstruction of bone and soft tissue falls under the maxillofacial umbrella.</p>
<p>By contrast, a general dentist works in an out-patient setting, usually with local anaesthesia. Their scope is restorative and preventive rather than reconstructive.</p>
<p><strong>What a general dentist typically does:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Check-ups, cleanings, fillings</li>
<li>Crowns, bridges, dentures</li>
<li>Simple extractions</li>
<li>Root canals (general dentists or endodontists)</li>
<li>Night guards for clenching</li>
<li>Whitening, veneers, basic cosmetic work</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What a maxillofacial surgeon typically does:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Impacted wisdom-tooth surgery</li>
<li>Dental implants and bone grafting</li>
<li>TMJ surgery (arthroscopy, joint replacement)</li>
<li>Orthognathic (jaw) surgery</li>
<li>Facial fracture repair</li>
<li>Oral cancer surgery and reconstruction</li>
<li>Cleft lip and palate surgery</li>
<li>Salivary-gland surgery</li>
<li>Facial cosmetic surgery related to the jaw and skeleton</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="when" class="scroll-mt-24">When to See Each One</h2>
<p>Use this as a practical decision guide. If your problem is in the teeth or gums and is routine, start with a dentist. If the problem involves the jaw bone, facial skeleton, deep surgical access, anaesthesia, or a suspicious lesion, go to a maxillofacial surgeon (or ask your dentist for a referral).</p>
<p><strong>See a dentist first for:</strong></p>
<p>See a <strong>dentist</strong> for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Toothache, decay, fillings</li>
<li>Cleanings and routine check-ups</li>
<li>Crowns, bridges, dentures</li>
<li>Simple extractions</li>
<li>Night guards for grinding</li>
</ul>
<p>See a <strong>maxillofacial surgeon</strong> for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Impacted wisdom teeth</li>
<li>Dental implants and bone grafts</li>
<li>TMJ disorders that don't respond to first-line treatment. See <a href="how-to-cure-tmj-permanently.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">how to cure TMJ permanently</a></li>
<li>Jaw deformity or asymmetry — see <a href="can-braces-fix-an-asymmetrical-jaw.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">braces vs surgery for asymmetry</a></li>
<li>Facial fractures — see <a href="broken-jaw-symptoms.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">broken jaw symptoms</a> and <a href="emergency-treatment-for-facial-injuries-near-hyderbaad.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">emergency treatment for facial injuries</a></li>
<li>Oral cancer or suspicious lesions — see <a href="what-are-the-symptoms-of-oral-cancer.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">oral cancer symptoms</a></li>
<li>Cysts and tumours of the jaw</li>
<li>Complications after dental surgery — see <a href="side-effects-of-maxillofacial-surgery-risks-complications-and-prevention.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">risks and complications of maxillofacial surgery</a></li>
<li>Impacted wisdom teeth that are symptomatic — see <a href="symptoms-of-impacted-wisdom-tooth.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">symptoms of impacted wisdom teeth</a> and <a href="can-wisdom-teeth-cause-tmj.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">can wisdom teeth cause TMJ</a></li>
<li>Cleft lip and palate</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="overlap" class="scroll-mt-24">Where They Overlap</h2>
<p>Even though the two roles are distinct, there is a real area of overlap — and that is healthy. Wisdom-tooth removal, single dental implants, and simple extractions can all be performed by either a general dentist or a maxillofacial surgeon, depending on training, complexity, and setting. The general rule is: if the case is straightforward and the dentist is trained and equipped, it stays with the dentist; if it is complex, deeply placed, involves bone, or carries risk to nearby nerves and sinuses, the case is referred to a maxillofacial surgeon.</p>
<p>The two work together all the time. For example:</p>
<p>The two work together all the time. For example:</p>
<ul>
<li>A dentist restores a tooth, and a maxillofacial surgeon places the implant</li>
<li>An orthodontist aligns the teeth, and a maxillofacial surgeon moves the jaws</li>
<li>A general dentist refers a patient with a complex problem to a maxillofacial surgeon</li>
<li>A maxillofacial surgeon manages jaw surgery, and the dentist manages the crowns and restorations</li>
</ul>
<p>For choosing the right one, see <a href="how-to-choose-the-best-maxillofacial-surgeon-in-hyderabad.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">how to choose a maxillofacial surgeon</a>.</p>
<h2 id="subspecialties" class="scroll-mt-24">Subspecialties Within Maxillofacial Surgery</h2>
<p>Maxillofacial surgery is itself a superspecialty, and most consultants further focus their practice. The recognised subspecialties include <strong>facial trauma</strong> (mandible, maxilla, orbital, nasal, and pan-facial fractures), <strong>orthognathic surgery</strong> (corrective jaw surgery for skeletal deformities, asymmetry, and sleep apnoea), <strong>head and neck oncology</strong> (oral cancer, salivary-gland tumours, neck dissection, and reconstruction with free flaps), <strong>cleft and craniofacial surgery</strong> (cleft lip and palate, syndromic craniosynostosis), <strong>TMJ surgery</strong> (arthroscopy, open joint surgery, total joint replacement), and <strong>facial cosmetic surgery</strong> (genioplasty, rhinoplasty as it relates to the jaw, scar revision, and facial recontouring).</p>
<p>Many maxillofacial surgeons also focus on <strong>implantology and full-mouth rehabilitation</strong>, combining bone grafting, sinus lifts, zygomatic implants, and soft-tissue management for complex missing-tooth cases. When you choose a surgeon, ask which subspecialty they focus on — a surgeon who does fifty orthognathic cases a year will give you a very different result from one who does fifty trauma cases.</p>
<div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-surface-container-high my-10"><img alt="Orthognathic surgical planning session with a maxillofacial team" class="w-full h-full object-cover" src="https://picsum.photos/1200/675?random=den-surgeon-3"/></div>
<h2 id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">Cost and Insurance in the Indian Context</h2>
<p>Costs differ sharply because the work is different. A routine dental visit — check-up, cleaning, single filling — typically runs from a few hundred to a couple of thousand rupees and is rarely covered by insurance. Crowns, bridges, root canals, and simple extractions fall in the low thousands. Specialist maxillofacial surgery — impacted wisdom-tooth removal under sedation, orthognathic surgery, facial fracture fixation, or oral cancer resection with reconstruction — runs into the tens of thousands to several lakhs, depending on the hospital, the implant hardware used, and whether general anaesthesia and an ICU stay are required.</p>
<p>Health insurance in India usually covers maxillofacial surgery when it is medically necessary: facial fractures from accidents, oral cancer, cyst and tumour removal, and corrective jaw surgery for documented skeletal deformity or obstructive sleep apnoea. Purely cosmetic facial surgery is generally excluded. Most policies also cover hospitalisation, anaesthesia, and implants used during reconstruction. Pre-authorisation is usually required for planned surgery, so confirm with your insurer and the hospital's TPA desk before the date of admission.</p>
<p>For emergency cases — a jaw fracture after a road accident, for example — most multispecialty hospitals in Hyderabad, Chennai, Bengaluru, Mumbai, and Delhi have a maxillofacial surgeon on call. Smaller cities may have an oral surgeon in a dental college attached to a medical college. If you are unsure where to go for a facial injury, read our guide on <a href="emergency-treatment-for-facial-injuries-near-hyderbaad.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">emergency treatment for facial injuries near Hyderabad</a>.</p>
